      <h2>free online vision test: what the screening actually covers</h2>
      <p>
        If you searched for a <strong>free online vision test</strong>, you are usually trying to
        answer one question: will I pass the vision check at the counter? This page lets you
        rehearse the whole procedure first — the same order of steps, the same 20/40 pass line, and
        the same tests the examiner runs — so that a machine you have never used is not the thing
        that decides your morning. A <strong>online vision test</strong> cannot replace the real
        examination, but a rehearsal removes the two things that most often cost people a second
        trip: not knowing the sequence, and turning up without their glasses. Can you get a
        <strong>free online vision test</strong> of charge? On this page, yes — the whole sequence
        runs in your browser, with no account, no fee and nothing uploaded.
      </p>

      <h2>What the DMV actually checks</h2>
      <p>
        A licence vision screen is short, and it is remarkably consistent across states. You will
        be asked to read the smallest line you can on a chart at a set distance, once with each eye
        and once with both, while wearing your normal correction. That is a <strong>Snellen
        test</strong>, and its arithmetic has not changed in a century: the letter subtends five
        arc-minutes per unit of acuity, so a 20/40 letter is twice the height of a 20/20 letter at
        the same distance. Most US states draw the unrestricted-licence pass line at
        <strong>20/40</strong>, which is what this screener uses as its benchmark.
      </p>
      <p>
        States that cannot rely on the Latin alphabet — or that test children and non-readers —
        use the <strong>Tumbling-E</strong> instead, where you report which way the open side of
        the E points. A field of vision check follows: a technician moves a target into your
        peripheral vision until you signal that you can see it, and the horizontal extent is
        recorded. Ordinary licences are typically measured against a <strong>130° to 140°</strong>
        horizontal field, roughly 70° to each side. Colour vision is screened for
        <strong>red versus green</strong> using Ishihara-style plates, because those are the
        signals a driver has to separate at speed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Commercial licences add a federal layer. Under 49 C.F.R. § 391.41 a <strong>CDL</strong>
        applicant needs <strong>20/40 corrected in each eye separately and in both together</strong>,
        <strong>70° of field in each eye and 130° together</strong>, and the ability to recognise
        <strong>red, green and amber</strong>. Individual performance on a regular licence does not
        carry over: a driver who comfortably passes 20/40 binocularly can still fail the
        per-eye requirement.
      </p>

      <h2>How the five biggest states differ</h2>
      <p>
        The pass line travels well, but the details do not. These are the five rows this site has
        checked against a primary source — the statute or the agency's own published standard.
      </p>
      <ul class="glance">
        <li><b>New York — 20/40.</b> The vision requirement sits in N.Y. Veh. &amp; Traf. Law § 502, and a provider can certify it on <b>form MV-619</b> instead of your reading the chart at a counter. If you searched for the <b>NYS online vision test</b> because you would rather do it at an optometrist's office, that form is the route.</li>
        <li><b>Florida — 20/40, then 20/70 with a 130° field.</b> Florida publishes a 130° horizontal field standard alongside its acuity line, so a field check that would never come up in some states is routine here.</li>
        <li><b>Illinois — 20/40, then 20/70 with a 140° field.</b> Illinois also stands out for age: from <b>75</b>, renewal means a road test as well as a vision check, and the cycle tightens.</li>
        <li><b>Texas — 20/40 with both eyes together.</b> The standard lives in <b>37 Tex. Admin. Code § 15.51</b>, and Texas reads it as a binocular requirement, which is friendlier than a per-eye one.</li>
        <li><b>California — 20/40, both eyes together.</b> California's requirement is in the Vehicle Code at <b>§ 12804.9</b> and is likewise treated as a binocular standard for the ordinary licence.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The practical lesson: read your own state's row in the
        <a href="#states">50-state table below</a> before you assume anything, and check the
        citation rather than a summary.
      </p>

      <h2>How to get an accurate result at home</h2>
      <p>
        Screen-based testing fails for one boring reason: nobody knows how big your pixels are.
        A chart displayed at "about the right size" can be 30 per cent off, which moves a 20/40
        line close enough to a 20/25 one to make the result meaningless. This screener solves that
        the way a clinic does — with a physical reference. Hold any bank card against the on-screen
        outline and slide until they match; a payment card is 85.6 mm wide by ISO/IEC 7810 ID-1, so
        the app can convert millimetres to pixels and draw a letter at its true size.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then set the room up properly. Stand at <strong>10 feet (3 m)</strong> — or 6 m if you have
        the space, which is the Snellen standard — mark the spot so you stand in the same place
        every time, dim the lights so the screen is the brightest thing in the room, and keep the
        screen at eye height. Cover one eye with your palm rather than pressing on it, and do not
        lean forward to rescue a line: the distance is the measurement.
      </p>

      <h2>The checks, one by one</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Distance acuity.</strong> The core test. An adaptive ladder walks from 20/200 down
        to 20/20, one eye at a time, and stops when you miss. Only as many letters as physically
        fit at their true size are drawn, so a phone shows a shorter line rather than a shrunken
        one.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Tumbling-E.</strong> Eight trials from 20/70 to 20/20, with the opening pointing
        in a random direction. Arrow keys work, which is how the test is delivered to people who
        cannot read the alphabet.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Colour vision.</strong> Fourteen synthetic Ishihara-style plates. Twelve correct is
        treated as normal; a missed plate tells you which part of the red-green axis to mention to
        an optometrist.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Peripheral awareness.</strong> Flashes appear at 60° in eight directions, and a
        clean run escalates to 75°, so a narrow field shows up as a pattern rather than a single
        missed dot.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Near vision.</strong> A reading card measured at 40 cm, using the same Snellen
        arithmetic, which is the part of the exam that catches people over 40.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The extras.</strong> Contrast sensitivity, an astigmatism dial and a duochrome
        refraction screen are not part of a licence check, but they are the reasons a person who
        "passes" still complains about night driving or print. They point at a direction, never at
        a prescription.
      </p>

      <h2>Why a calibrated chart beats a picture of a chart</h2>
      <p>
        Many sites show an image of a Snellen chart and ask you to read it. It looks the same, but
        it measures nothing: the image is a fixed number of pixels, so the visual angle depends
        entirely on your monitor and how far you sit from it. Two people can read the same JPEG and
        be told different things about their eyes. A calibrated chart is the only kind that can
        claim to reproduce a physical test, which is why the card step here is mandatory rather
        than optional — and why the letters are drawn as vector optotypes on a 5x5 grid instead of
        set in a font, so the stroke-to-height ratio stays correct at every size.
      </p>

      <h2>What to bring to the counter</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Your glasses or contacts, even if you only wear them for driving. Uncorrected testing is the single most common reason for a re-test.</li>
        <li>Any prescription you have, or the name of the optometrist who wrote it, if your state accepts provider certification such as New York's MV-619.</li>
        <li>Your current licence or ID, proof of identity, and proof of residency if your state requires it for a renewal.</li>
        <li>Five spare minutes. The vision check itself is quick; the queue is not.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>CDL, seniors and renewals</h2>
      <p>
        Commercial drivers should rehearse the federal set specifically — per-eye acuity, the
        Tumbling-E for the field requirement, and a colour run — because the pass line is stricter
        and the tests are run separately. Older drivers face a different wrinkle: several states
        shorten the renewal cycle with age, and Illinois adds a road test from 75. New York will
        accept an eye-care provider's certification on form MV-619, which is often easier to
        arrange than a slot at a busy office. Whatever your age, the useful preparation is the
        same: know your numbers before you are asked for them.
      </p>

      <h2>Practice tips</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Do the whole sequence, not one test. Fatigue and unfamiliarity are what make a second attempt worse than the first.</li>
        <li>Re-calibrate if you change monitor, zoom level or browser window size. A zoomed page is an uncalibrated page.</li>
        <li>Write your result down. Comparing this month's line with last month's is more informative than either number alone.</li>
        <li>Test at the same time of day. Tears, screen glare and tiredness all move the answer.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What this screener is not</h2>
      <p>
        It is not an official DMV or DOT test, not a medical examination, and it cannot diagnose a
        condition or produce a prescription. Screen-based results depend on your display, your
        lighting and your distance, and the state summaries are a guide that may be out of date.
        Treat it as a rehearsal: it tells you roughly where you stand and what to expect, and it
        tells you when to book a real appointment.
      </p>

